---
import accessKeyingSvg from "../../../../docs/diagrams/access-and-keying.svg?raw";
import infrastructureSvg from "../../../../docs/diagrams/infrastructure.svg?raw";
import DocsDiagram from "../components/DocsDiagram.astro";
import PageHeader from "../components/PageHeader.astro";
import SectionHeader from "../components/SectionHeader.astro";
import SecurityArchitectureDiagram from "../components/SecurityArchitectureDiagram.astro";
import SecurityCryptography from "../components/SecurityCryptography.astro";
import SecurityLimits from "../components/SecurityLimits.astro";
import SecuritySharingDiagram from "../components/SecuritySharingDiagram.astro";
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { docPath, sourceUrl, technicalDocs } from "../technicalDocs";
import "../styles/security.css";
import "../styles/securityDiagrams.css";

// Section ids are linked from Home, Features, and older URLs: keep them stable.
const contents = [
  { href: "#threat-model", label: "Assumptions" },
  { href: "#access-keying", label: "Access and keys" },
  { href: "#device-boundary", label: "Device and service" },
  { href: "#verified-sharing", label: "Verified sharing" },
  { href: "#cryptography", label: "Cryptography" },
  { href: "#infrastructure", label: "Infrastructure" },
  { href: "#trust-boundaries", label: "Scope" },
  { href: "#documents", label: "Technical documents" },
];

const zanzibarPaperUrl =
  "https://www.usenix.org/conference/atc19/presentation/pang";
---

<Layout
  title="Security | Tearleads"
  description="How Tearleads encrypts content on your device, how the app verifies signed access records, what the sync service can see, and the scope of each guarantee."
>
  <div class="site-page">
    <PageHeader
      title="Security"
      lede="Your content is encrypted on your device. Sharing is backed by signed access rules: the app verifies the signed policies, access manifests, and write signatures on the records it uses."
    >
      <p class="security-header-note">
        This page summarizes the implementation.
        The <a href="#documents">technical documents</a> define the rules and
        known gaps in detail.
      </p>
    </PageHeader>

    <nav class="site-contents security-contents" aria-label="On this page">
      <ol role="list">
        {contents.map((item, index) => (
          <li>
            <a href={item.href}>
              <span class="site-index" aria-hidden="true">{String(index + 1).padStart(2, "0")}</span>{item.label}
            </a>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>
    </nav>

    <section class="site-section security-section security-section--first" id="threat-model" aria-labelledby="threat-model-title">
      <SectionHeader index="01" titleId="threat-model-title" title="Assumptions" />
      <p class="site-prose">
        These properties assume an honest, uncompromised client that runs its
        validation checks, uncompromised user private keys, authentic identity
        keys, and working cryptographic primitives. The API and its storage may
        be compromised. What falls outside these guarantees is listed under
        <a href="#trust-boundaries">Scope</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="site-section security-section" id="access-keying" aria-labelledby="access-keying-title">
      <SectionHeader
        index="02"
        titleId="access-keying-title"
        title="Access lookup and keying"
        lede="Authority comes from signed manifests. Access is looked up from a resource up to the people it is shared with; keys unwrap the other way, from a user key down to the content."
      />
      <div class="site-prose">
        <p>
          The permission structure draws on Google's <a href={zanzibarPaperUrl}>Zanzibar</a>:
          access is recorded as relationships, and a check starts at the resource and
          walks upward. A file is reachable through the documents it is attached
          to, a document through every container (a folder in Explorer) it is
          linked into, and a container through its parents up to the root. One
          document can therefore be shared through a mix of grants applied at
          different depths of the container hierarchy, and those grants add up.
        </p>
        <p>
          Unlike Zanzibar, clients do not take the server's answer on trust. The
          API enforces access, but each relationship is also a signed record
          that every client verifies for itself before it reads or encrypts,
          and Tearleads uses a fixed set of relationships rather than
          Zanzibar's configurable rewrite rules. Keys follow the same paths in
          reverse: a container's key opens the keys of the containers,
          documents, and files beneath it.
        </p>
      </div>
      <DocsDiagram svg={accessKeyingSvg} label="Access lookup and keying diagram" captionId="access-keying-caption">
        <span class="security-figure-label">Fig. 1</span> Bob can read Blob B because it is
        bound to Doc D, which links Inbox (C3), where Bob holds a direct write grant.
        Solid lines are signed lookup relationships; dashed lines are key unwraps.
        The <a class="site-link" href={sourceUrl("docs/access-plane.md")}>access plane document</a>
        describes each rule.
      </DocsDiagram>
    </section>

    <section class="site-section security-section" id="device-boundary" aria-labelledby="device-title">
      <SectionHeader
        index="03"
        titleId="device-title"
        title="Your device and the sync service"
        lede="Local encryption protects data stored on your device. End-to-end content encryption lets the service sync documents and files without the keys needed to read them."
      />
      <SecurityArchitectureDiagram />
      <div class="security-pair">
        <div>
          <h3>Encrypted</h3>
          <ul class="security-inventory" role="list">
            <li>Document and file contents</li>
            <li>Encrypted metadata, such as folder names</li>
            <li>Organization names and custom group names</li>
            <li>The keys wrapped for recipients</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Decrypting them requires the matching private keys or unwrapped
            content keys.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Visible to the service</h3>
          <ul class="security-inventory" role="list">
            <li>Built-in group roles, such as Admins and Members</li>
            <li>Roster and group membership</li>
            <li>
              The structure of folders, documents, and files, including which
              attachments belong to which documents
            </li>
            <li>Identifiers and public keys</li>
            <li>Access-policy and grant relationships</li>
            <li>
              Which user and device signed each access change and write, with
              its signed timestamp
            </li>
            <li>The metadata that orders document updates</li>
            <li>Ciphertext sizes and storage usage</li>
            <li>
              IP addresses, session and activity times, and the IP address
              recorded when your identity is registered
            </li>
            <li>
              Subscription and billing records, including the billing email for
              a web subscription
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Encryption does not hide this information or traffic patterns such
            as request timing and size.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="site-section security-section" id="verified-sharing" aria-labelledby="sharing-title">
      <SectionHeader
        index="04"
        titleId="sharing-title"
        title="Verified sharing"
        lede="A container (a folder in Explorer) groups content and its access rules. Clients derive who can receive its keys from verified access manifests and signed group policies. Editing a server-side membership row is not enough to grant access."
      />
      <SecuritySharingDiagram />
      <div class="security-pair">
        <div>
          <h3>Signed history and checkpoints</h3>
          <p>
            Signatures and hash chains bind policies and access changes to their
            authors and predecessors. Clients remember identity keys and policy
            and access checkpoints, and reject later substitutions, conflicting
            versions of the same history, and rollbacks below the state they
            have seen.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Revocation applies going forward</h3>
          <p>
            Removing group members rotates the group key and the affected
            container keys in one atomic update. Removing access cannot erase
            content or keys someone already received. Nested containers and
            retained history are covered under <a href="#trust-boundaries">Scope</a>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <SecurityCryptography />

    <section class="site-section security-section" id="infrastructure" aria-labelledby="infrastructure-title">
      <SectionHeader
        index="06"
        titleId="infrastructure-title"
        title="Infrastructure"
        lede="The sync service runs on Cloudflare Workers with PlanetScale Postgres and Cloudflare R2 storage. Every part of it sits on the service side of the boundary in Fig. 2."
      />
      <DocsDiagram svg={infrastructureSvg} label="Infrastructure diagram" captionId="infrastructure-caption">
        <span class="security-figure-label">Fig. 4</span> The production tier. Clients
        talk to the API Worker and fetch encrypted blob bytes from R2 through
        short-lived presigned URLs. Dashed boxes are third-party services. The
        <a class="site-link" href={sourceUrl("docs/developer/infrastructure.md")}>infrastructure guide</a>
        describes each stack and deployment step.
      </DocsDiagram>
    </section>

    <SecurityLimits />

    <section class="site-section security-section" id="documents" aria-labelledby="documents-title">
      <SectionHeader
        index="08"
        titleId="documents-title"
        title="Technical documents"
        lede="These documents, published from the public Tearleads repository on GitHub, define the verification rules, assumptions, and remaining gaps in more detail."
      />
      <dl class="site-spec site-spec--wide-key security-documents">
        {technicalDocs.map((doc) => (
          <div>
            <dt>
              <a class="site-link" href={docPath(doc)}>{doc.title}</a>
            </dt>
            <dd>
              {doc.summary}
              <span class="security-documents-path">{doc.source}</span>
            </dd>
          </div>
        ))}
      </dl>
    </section>
  </div>
</Layout>
